# 7. CONSTITUTION-BUILDING WORKFLOW

## 7.1 Classroom creation

Teachers create a classroom by providing:

- Classroom name.
- Unique classroom code.
- Constitution drafting deadline.
- Optional classroom description.

Example classroom:
- Name: Class 7A
- Code: ET01
- Deadline: 30 November 2026

The teacher should be able to view and manage the classroom.

## 7.2 Initial constitution drafting

Before the initial constitution is approved, students can propose:

- Rights.
- Duties.
- Articles.

The proposal form must include:

- Category.
- Proposal title.
- Proposed content.
- Rationale: Why should this be included in the classroom constitution?

The form should provide simple Marathi guidance.

Example:
- Title: सर्वांना समान संधी
- Content: प्रत्येक विद्यार्थ्याला वर्गातील उपक्रमांमध्ये सहभागी होण्याची समान संधी मिळावी.
- Rationale: प्रत्येक विद्यार्थ्याला स्वतःचे कौशल्य दाखवता यावे.

Students must be able to view existing proposals and avoid unnecessary duplicate submissions.

The teacher can close the initial drafting period after the deadline or manually.

The deadline should be visible to students.

## 7.3 Proposal discussion

Every proposal must have a dedicated detail page.

Display:
- Proposal title.
- Category.
- Author.
- Proposed content.
- Rationale.
- Current status.
- Like count.
- Dislike count.
- Discussion section.
- Formal voting information, when applicable.

Students can:
- Like.
- Dislike.
- Remove or change their previous informal reaction.
- Add comments.
- Reply to comments.

Only authenticated, enrolled students and the relevant teacher should access classroom discussions.

Keep discussion simple and readable on mobile.

## 7.4 Formal voting

Formal voting must be separate from likes and dislikes.

Voting requirements:
- Teacher opens formal voting.
- Only enrolled students can vote.
- Each student gets one formal vote per proposal per voting round.
- Students can choose Approve or Reject.
- Students can see voting status and final results.
- Students cannot vote after voting is closed.

Calculate the majority using formal votes only.

Use this rule:

A proposal passes when:

Approve votes > Reject votes.

Examples:
- 12 approve and 8 reject: passes.
- 10 approve and 10 reject: no majority.
- 7 approve and 13 reject: fails.

A proposal that passes majority voting moves to teacher review.

A proposal that does not receive majority support should be marked as not passed and should not automatically enter teacher approval.

Show the number of votes and outcome clearly.

## 7.5 Teacher decision workflow

After a proposal receives majority support, the teacher can choose:

**Approve**
- The proposal becomes part of the active constitution.
- Create a constitutional provision.
- Update the constitution dashboard.
- Record the approval and timestamp.

**Reject**
- The proposal is rejected.
- The teacher must provide a reason.
- Students can see the decision and explanation.

**Reconsider**
- Return the proposal to discussion or revision.
- Provide a reason for reconsideration.
- Allow the student to revise the proposal if appropriate.

**Veto**
- Block the proposal.
- Require a written justification.
- Record the decision.

Display the teacher's decision and explanation on the proposal detail page.

Only approved provisions should appear as active constitutional content.

